#lang scribble/base
@; -*- Scheme -*-
@(require "util/ltuo_lib.rkt")
@(set-chapter-number 12)

@title[#:tag "AB" #:style 'unnumbered]{Annotated Bibliography}
@tex{\markboth{ANNOTATED BIBLIOGRAPHY}{ANNOTATED BIBLIOGRAPHY}}
@epigraph{Buying books would be a good thing if one could also buy the time to read them.
  @|#:- "Arthur Schopenhauer"|
}
I absolutely loathe bibliographies that are just walls of references,
without any guidance as to what matters about each work referred to.
Which of those hundreds of entries are relevant to which questions?
What was good or bad about each entry? What made it worth citing?
What are its notable achievements—especially the underrated gems?
What traps did the author fall into that a reader should be wary of?
What historical context makes it important?
Which of the now-obvious concepts it discusses were then non-obvious innovations?
What crucial words shifted in meaning since it was written?
What concepts does it touch that were only fully identified later?
What does the citing author think of the cited work, and
how does that differ from more common opinions of said work?
Should I read the work or not? If not, what should I know of it?
If yes, what need I know to make the best of the reading?

As an author, I need to have those notes anyway, for my own use,
because there are far too many entries for me to remember the details of each.
Many of those writings, I even want to forget,
to make space for more worthy readings in my quite limited @emph{brain attic};
yet not without making a note of what was notable, so I never have to re-read them,
even if I need to revisit the topic—which I definitely do many times over while writing.
I deem it my duty as an author to share with my readers
what useful notes I have about each entry.
I invite other authors to do as much.

My notes are opinionated. They skip over aspects of cited works that I didn’t care about,
at least not for this book.
They also pass summary judgment upon some authors and their works, based on
what limited information I could gather and make sense of—@emph{strong opinions, weakly held}.
I’ll be happy to be proven wrong, or to be shown nuances I missed.
Yet those are the best opinions I could form, and still worth sharing.
@principle{Always judge a book by its cover}:
that’s the only information you have before you may decide to even open the book.
But be ready to revise your judgment after you get more information.

All publications listed below are available online for free—but
not always based on their DOI (Digital Object Identifier).
Surely my astute readers can circumvent “legal” monopoly middlemen to locate free copies.
Hint: my source code got URLs.

@(hhr)

@(generate-bibliography #:sec-title #f)
@(report-unused-bibtex)
